R2Compare — proposed fix P1 (tool, residual sentinel collision)
Target: tools/kama-live-replay.py (the lane tool; as-built at 380bcdff and the
uncommitted F11 worktree version both have this residual).
Problem: the fallback value `"unparseable"` (or a recorded body_sha256 string) can
equal a *parsed* body value, so a receipt pair in which the models body changes
from "parses to the string 'unparseable' (or to a given 64-hex digest)" to
"unparseable (with that digest / no digest)" is judged EQUAL by the tool while the
frozen counter's projection judges it DIFFERENT (demonstrated: pair-worktree-
collision-* exit 0; pair-worktree-digeststr-* exit 0). A Python tuple is a value
no json.loads output can be, so it cannot collide with a parsed value.

  --- a/tools/kama-live-replay.py
  +++ b/tools/kama-live-replay.py
  @@ -175,7 +175,7 @@ def projection(rec):
       if body is None and raw is not None:
           # unparseable or non-string body: fall back to the byte digest so two
           # different unparseable bodies do not compare equal (F11)
  -        models = _get(rec, "captures", "models", "body_sha256") or "unparseable"
  +        models = ("unparseable", _get(rec, "captures", "models", "body_sha256"))

Sanity under the proposed semantics:
- two different unparseable bodies with recorded digests -> tuples differ -> DIFF (unchanged)
- two unparseable bodies without digests -> ('unparseable', None) == ('unparseable', None) -> EQ (unchanged)
- body parsing to the string "unparseable" vs unparseable body without digest
  -> "unparseable" vs ('unparseable', None) -> DIFF (was EQ; fixed)
- body parsing to a 64-hex string vs unparseable body with that digest
  -> digest-string vs ('unparseable', digest-string) -> DIFF (was EQ; fixed)
